<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-milnor-descent-and-strong-sheafiness">
  <title>Milnor descent and strong sheafiness</title>
  <p>
    We first state the general descent result proved in the formalisation.  It is
a result about local section rings attached to rational-localisation data, rather than about an
arbitrary cartesian square of global rings.
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-milnor-sheaf-transfer">
    <title>Milnor descent</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R,B,C,D</m> be the underlying rings of complete nonarchimedean Huber pairs,
with continuous maps from <m>R</m> to <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>D</m>, and from <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> to
<m>D</m>.  Suppose that rational-localisation data on <m>R</m> can be carried functorially to the
other three vertices, that the resulting maps on section rings commute, and
that this construction transports rational covers, is natural under
refinement, and carries matching families to compatible families on common
refinements.  Suppose also that for every rational-localisation datum <m>U</m> the map
<me>\mathcal O_R(U)\longrightarrow
  \mathcal O_B(U_B)\oplus\mathcal O_C(U_C)</me>
is a topological embedding whose image is the equaliser of the two maps to
<m>\mathcal O_D(U_D)</m>, compatibly with restriction and matching families.  If
<m>B,C,D</m> are sheafy, then <m>R</m> is sheafy.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="870d0eed2c48a020109d766d2af89c3f47469a94" ref="ValuationSpectrum.isSheafy_of_milnorSquare">Lean</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Push a finite rational cover of <m>U</m> to the other vertices.  Separation over
<m>B</m> and <m>C</m>, followed by injectivity of the map in the theorem, gives separation
over <m>R</m>.  A matching family glues over <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>; the two sections have the
same image over <m>D</m>, and the equaliser hypothesis gives the unique section
over <m>R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the topology, the restriction map for <m>R</m> factors, through the
topological embedding in the theorem, into the product of the restriction maps for <m>B</m> and
<m>C</m>.  Those maps are topological embeddings because <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are sheafy.
The factorisation therefore shows that the restriction map for <m>R</m> is also a
topological embedding.  This is the topological sheaf condition on finite
rational covers; the rational-basis theorem gives the sheaf condition on all
opens <xref ref="bib-Stacks" detail="Tag 009O"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <corollary xml:id="thm-sheafy">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every <m>s\geq0</m> and every ring of integral elements
<me>A_s^+\subseteq A\langle V_1,\ldots,V_s\rangle^\circ,</me>
the Huber pair
<me>\bigl(A\langle V_1,\ldots,V_s\rangle,A_s^+\bigr)</me>
is sheafy.  In particular, <m>A</m> is strongly sheafy.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr">Lean (isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="870d0eed2c48a020109d766d2af89c3f47469a94" ref="FiniteJet.finiteJet_tateExt_isSheafyComplete">Lean (finiteJet_tateExt_isSheafyComplete)</lean>
  </corollary>
  <p>
    The proof below works over the complete discretely valued base fixed in the
introduction.  The pinned Lean formalisation of this strong form currently
covers the Laurent-series model over <m>F((t))</m>; ordinary sheafiness is
formalised over an arbitrary complete discretely valued base.
  </p>
  <proof>
    <p>
      The estimates <xref ref="eq-structural-map-bounds"/> make the structural maps
morphisms of Huber pairs.  The rings <m>B,C,D</m> are strongly noetherian affinoid
<m>k</m>-algebras and hence sheafy by Huber's theorem
<xref ref="bib-Huber94" detail="Theorem 2.2"/>.  <xref ref="prop-localized-milnor"/>, together
with its compatibility under refinement, supplies the hypotheses of
<xref ref="thm-milnor-sheaf-transfer"/>.  This proves sheafiness for <m>A</m>, independently
of the choice of its ring of integral elements.
    </p>
    <p>
      After adjoining <m>V_1,\ldots,V_s</m>, the coefficientwise split square and the
localised equaliser rows persist, while the other three vertices remain
strongly noetherian.  Applying the same theorem proves the assertion for every
<m>s</m> and every <m>A_s^+</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <proof>
    <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-main"/></title>
    <p>
      <xref ref="prop-uniform-domain"/> proves that <m>A</m> is complete, uniform,
non-noetherian, and a domain.  Strong sheafiness is <xref ref="thm-sheafy"/>, and
failure of stable uniformity is <xref ref="prop-not-stably-uniform"/>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>
